---
import LessonLayout from '../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
---

<LessonLayout
  title='Kotlin syntax — the foundations'
  description='val/var, types, control flow as expressions, ranges, loops, default and named arguments. The pieces that show up in every program.'
  number=1
  primary='#2d3142'
  accent='#ef8354'
>
  <div class="eyebrow">Kotlin · Foundations</div>
    <h1>Kotlin syntax, as <em>one</em> philosophy: less ceremony, more intent.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      <code>val</code>/<code>var</code>, types, control flow, ranges, loops, function
      args — they look like six separate topics, but they're really six ways the
      language removes bookkeeping so your code reads as the <strong>intent</strong>,
      not the steps.
    </p>

    <div class="meta">
      <span>⏱ ~11 min read</span>
      <span>🎯 Absolute beginner → early intermediate</span>
      <span>🧠 The through-line: every feature removes ceremony</span>
    </div>

    <!-- THE BIG QUESTION -->
    <div class="question">
      <div class="question-label">The one question behind the whole page</div>
      <p class="question-text">
        What if the language <em>removed</em> every piece of syntax whose only job
        was to satisfy the compiler — and kept only the syntax that describes
        <strong>what you meant</strong>?
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- THE CEREMONY-REDUCTION MAP -->
    <div class="reduce">
      <div class="reduce-title">The same six features, framed as "what ceremony disappears"</div>
      <table class="reduce-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Feature</th>
            <th>What it makes unnecessary</th>
            <th>What you write instead</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="feat">val (default)</td>
            <td><span class="gone">`final` keyword · getter boilerplate</span></td>
            <td><span class="kept">`val name: String`</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="feat">Type inference</td>
            <td><span class="gone">Redundant type annotations the compiler already knows</span></td>
            <td><span class="kept">`val n = 5` instead of `val n: Int = 5`</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="feat">if/when/try as expressions</td>
            <td><span class="gone">Temporary variables assigned in each branch</span></td>
            <td><span class="kept">`val x = if (...) a else b`</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="feat">Ranges + `in`</td>
            <td><span class="gone">`i = 0; while (i < 10) ...; i++` ceremony</span></td>
            <td><span class="kept">`for (i in 1..10) ...`</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="feat">for (x in xs)</td>
            <td><span class="gone">Iterator protocol · index arithmetic · off-by-one worry</span></td>
            <td><span class="kept">`for (u in users) ...`</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="feat">Default + named args</td>
            <td><span class="gone">Overload explosion · "what was that second bool?"</span></td>
            <td><span class="kept">`connect(timeout = 5_000, retries = 3)`</span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Now walk through each one. They're the same idea — <em>let the language do
      the bookkeeping, so your code says what it means</em> — applied to six
      different parts of writing a program.
    </p>

    <!-- 1. VAL/VAR -->
    <h2><span class="num">1</span>val vs var — pick the one that matches reality</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="valvar">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M7 7h10"/><path d="M7 12h10"/><path d="M7 17h6"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">val — read-only. var — mutable.</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">the reference is what val/var controls · not the object it points to</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="concept-tagline">
        <code>val</code> means "this name will always refer to the same object."
        <code>var</code> means "I intend to reassign it later." The default is
        <code>val</code>. Use <code>var</code> only when reality requires it.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">name</span> = <span class="s">"Mavis"</span>          <span class="c">// can't reassign later — compile error</span>
  <span class="k">var</span> <span class="v">counter</span> = <span class="n">0</span>             <span class="c">// can reassign: counter++ is fine</span>

  name = <span class="s">"Other"</span>   <span class="c">// ❌ Val cannot be reassigned</span>
  counter++            <span class="c">// ✅ var allows it</span>

  <span class="c">// val refers to the BINDING, not the object. The object can still mutate:</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">list</span> = <span class="fn">mutableListOf</span>(<span class="n">1</span>, <span class="n">2</span>, <span class="n">3</span>)
  list.<span class="fn">add</span>(<span class="n">4</span>)   <span class="c">// ✅ list still refers to the same MutableList, which now has 4 items</span>
  list = <span class="fn">mutableListOf</span>()   <span class="c">// ❌ can't reassign the val</span></div>

      <div class="ok">
        <strong>Default rule:</strong> reach for <code>val</code>. Switch to <code>var</code>
        only when you have a concrete reason. The compiler will warn you if a
        <code>var</code> could be a <code>val</code> — listen to it.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 2. TYPES -->
    <h2><span class="num">2</span>types — declare them when they add information</h2>

    <div class="concept" data-t="types">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 7h16"/><path d="M4 12h16"/><path d="M4 17h10"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">types</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">Basic types · inference · when to be explicit</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="concept-tagline">
        Kotlin has all the basic types you'd expect. The headline feature is
        <strong>type inference</strong>: you only write the type when it adds
        information. Most of the time, the compiler can read it from the
        right-hand side.
      </p>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// Basic types</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">i</span>: <span class="t">Int</span> = <span class="n">42</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">l</span>: <span class="t">Long</span> = <span class="n">42L</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">d</span>: <span class="t">Double</span> = <span class="n">3.14</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">b</span>: <span class="t">Boolean</span> = <span class="k">true</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">c</span>: <span class="t">Char</span> = <span class="s">'x'</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> <span class="v">s</span>: <span class="t">String</span> = <span class="s">"hello"</span>

  <span class="c">// Inference: drop the type when it's obvious</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> n = <span class="n">42</span>           <span class="c">// Int, inferred</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> pi = <span class="n">3.14</span>        <span class="c">// Double, inferred</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> msg = <span class="s">"hi"</span>       <span class="c">// String, inferred</span>

  <span class="c">// String templates — interpolate with $ or $&#123;&#125;</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> greeting = <span class="s">"Hello, </span><span class="th">$name</span><span class="s">! Next year you'll be </span><span class="th">$&#123;</span>age + <span class="n">1</span><span class="th">&#125;</span><span class="s">."</span></div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>When to write the type explicitly:</strong> public function signatures
        (callers benefit), nullable types (<code>String?</code> — the <code>?</code> matters),
        and anything the compiler can't infer (e.g. you need a parent type, or the
        initializer is delayed).
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 3. CONTROL FLOW AS EXPRESSIONS -->
    <h2><span class="num">3</span>control flow as expressions — the biggest mental shift</h2>

    <p>
      In most languages, <code>if</code> and <code>switch</code> are <em>statements</em>:
      they do something. In Kotlin, they're <em>expressions</em>: they produce a
      value. Same for <code>try</code>. This single change eliminates a huge class
      of "assign a temporary in each branch" code.
    </p>

    <h3><code>if</code> as expression</h3>

    <div class="pair">
      <div>
        <div class="pair-label bad">Statement-style (other languages)</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="t">String</span> result;
  <span class="k">if</span> (score &gt;= <span class="n">60</span>) &#123;
      result = <span class="s">"pass"</span>;
  &#125; <span class="k">else</span> &#123;
      result = <span class="s">"fail"</span>;
  &#125;</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="pair-label good">Expression-style (Kotlin)</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> result = <span class="k">if</span> (score &gt;= <span class="n">60</span>) <span class="s">"pass"</span> <span class="k">else</span> <span class="s">"fail"</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="concept" data-t="if">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><polyline points="9 14 4 9 9 4"/><path d="M20 20v-7a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H4"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">if / else — returns the chosen value</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">val x = if (cond) a else b · no temporary needed</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The whole <code>if/else</code> block is a value. The compiler checks that
        every path produces something. The "no else" branch implicitly returns
        <code>Unit</code> — Kotlin's "no useful return" type.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h3><code>when</code> as expression</h3>

    <p>
      <code>when</code> is Kotlin's switch replacement — but it returns a value, it
      matches more than just constants (ranges, types, arbitrary expressions), and
      you don't need explicit <code>break</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="concept" data-t="when">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><path d="M12 3v18"/><path d="M3 12h18"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">when — the multi-arm expression</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">when (x) &#123; 1 -> ... ; in 2..10 -> ... ; is String -> ... &#125;</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// As an expression — the whole thing is a value</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> label = <span class="k">when</span> (status) &#123;
      <span class="v">PENDING</span> -&gt; <span class="s">"Waiting"</span>
      <span class="v">SHIPPED</span> -&gt; <span class="s">"On the way"</span>
      <span class="v">DELIVERED</span> -&gt; <span class="s">"Arrived"</span>
      <span class="v">CANCELLED</span> -&gt; <span class="s">"Done"</span>
  &#125;

  <span class="c">// `when` as a STATEMENT (no value, just side effects) is also valid:</span>
  <span class="k">when</span> (status) &#123;
      <span class="v">PENDING</span> -&gt; <span class="fn">notifyUser</span>()
      <span class="v">SHIPPED</span> -&gt; <span class="fn">startTracking</span>()
      <span class="k">else</span> -&gt; &#123;&#125;   <span class="c">// not required when used as a statement</span>
  &#125;</div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>When used as an expression, <code>when</code> must be exhaustive.</strong>
        The compiler will tell you if a case is missing. This is the same
        "impossible states" idea from the sealed class lesson — <code>when</code>
        over a sealed class is a compile-time check.
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3><code>try</code> as expression</h3>

    <div class="concept" data-t="try">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 9v4"/><path d="M12 17h.01"/><path d="M10.29 3.86 1.82 18a2 2 0 0 0 1.71 3h16.94a2 2 0 0 0 1.71-3L13.71 3.86a2 2 0 0 0-3.42 0z"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">try / catch — also returns a value</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">val n = try &#123; parse(s) &#125; catch (e: Exception) &#123; -1 &#125;</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// The whole try/catch produces a value — last expression wins</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> n = <span class="k">try</span> &#123;
      s.<span class="fn">toInt</span>()
  &#125; <span class="k">catch</span> (e: <span class="t">NumberFormatException</span>) &#123;
      -<span class="n">1</span>
  &#125;

  <span class="c">// try can also have a `finally` — but finally doesn't contribute to the value</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="ok">
      <strong>The mental model:</strong> if a block has a "last expression," that
      expression is the block's value. <code>if</code>, <code>when</code>, and
      <code>try</code> all follow this rule. You stop needing temporary variables
      to hold a result across branches.
    </div>

    <!-- 4. RANGES -->
    <h2><span class="num">4</span>ranges — "from X to Y" is a first-class value</h2>

    <p>
      Kotlin makes "a span of integers" a real object. You build them with
      operators, use them with <code>in</code> / <code>!in</code>, and iterate
      them with <code>for</code>. No <code>i &lt; n</code> ceremony, no off-by-one
      worry unless you actually want one.
    </p>

    <div class="ranges">
      <div class="range-row">
        <div class="range-syntax">1..5</div>
        <div class="range-viz">
          <div class="range-cell edge">1</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">2</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">3</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">4</div>
          <div class="range-cell edge">5</div>
        </div>
        <div class="range-desc"><strong>Inclusive</strong> on both ends. <code>1..5</code> is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="range-row">
        <div class="range-syntax">1 until 5</div>
        <div class="range-viz">
          <div class="range-cell edge">1</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">2</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">3</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">4</div>
          <div class="range-cell">5</div>
        </div>
        <div class="range-desc"><strong>End-exclusive.</strong> 1, 2, 3, 4 — useful for "index from 0 to size."</div>
      </div>

      <div class="range-row">
        <div class="range-syntax">1..10 step 2</div>
        <div class="range-viz">
          <div class="range-cell edge">1</div>
          <div class="range-cell">2</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">3</div>
          <div class="range-cell">4</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">5</div>
          <div class="range-cell">6</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">7</div>
          <div class="range-cell">8</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">9</div>
          <div class="range-cell edge">10</div>
        </div>
        <div class="range-desc"><strong>With stride.</strong> Every nth value.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="range-row">
        <div class="range-syntax">5 downTo 1</div>
        <div class="range-viz">
          <div class="range-cell edge">5</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">4</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">3</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">2</div>
          <div class="range-cell edge">1</div>
        </div>
        <div class="range-desc"><strong>Reverse.</strong> 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="range-row">
        <div class="range-syntax">'a'..'z'</div>
        <div class="range-viz">
          <div class="range-cell edge">a</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">b</div>
          <div class="range-cell">…</div>
          <div class="range-cell in">y</div>
          <div class="range-cell edge">z</div>
        </div>
        <div class="range-desc"><strong>Char ranges work too</strong> — and so do custom <code>Comparable</code> types.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// `in` and `!in` are range-membership operators</span>
  <span class="k">if</span> (score <span class="k">in</span> <span class="n">0</span>..<span class="n">100</span>) <span class="fn">println</span>(<span class="s">"valid"</span>)
  <span class="k">if</span> (ch <span class="k">!</span><span class="k">in</span> <span class="s">'a'</span>..<span class="s">'z'</span>) <span class="fn">println</span>(<span class="s">"not lowercase"</span>)

  <span class="c">// Ranges are objects — you can name them, pass them around</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> validAges = <span class="n">0</span>..<span class="n">120</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> isValid = age <span class="k">in</span> validAges</div>

    <!-- 5. LOOPS -->
    <h2><span class="num">5</span>loops — "do this for each thing"</h2>

    <p>
      Kotlin has the loop shapes you'd expect, but <code>for</code> is the
      workhorse. It doesn't loop a counter — it iterates over anything that has
      an iterator (lists, sets, maps, ranges, strings, files).
    </p>

    <div class="concept" data-t="loops">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M21 12a9 9 0 1 1-9-9c2.52 0 4.93 1 6.74 2.74L21 8"/><path d="M21 3v5h-5"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">for · while · repeat</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">three loop shapes, each for a different job</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// for — iterate over anything iterable</span>
  <span class="k">for</span> (u <span class="k">in</span> users) <span class="fn">println</span>(u.name)
  <span class="k">for</span> (i <span class="k">in</span> <span class="n">1</span>..<span class="n">5</span>) <span class="fn">println</span>(i)           <span class="c">// 1, 2, 3, 4, 5</span>
  <span class="k">for</span> (i <span class="k">in</span> <span class="n">0</span> until <span class="v">users</span>.<span class="v">size</span>) <span class="fn">println</span>(users[i])   <span class="c">// 0 to size-1</span>
  <span class="k">for</span> ((k, v) <span class="k">in</span> map) <span class="fn">println</span>(<span class="s">"</span><span class="th">$k</span><span class="s"> = </span><span class="th">$v</span><span class="s">"</span>)   <span class="c">// destructuring for maps</span>
  <span class="k">for</span> ((i, u) <span class="k">in</span> users.<span class="fn">withIndex</span>()) <span class="fn">println</span>(<span class="s">"</span><span class="th">$i</span><span class="s">: </span><span class="th">$u</span><span class="s">"</span>)   <span class="c">// index + element</span>

  <span class="c">// while — when you don't know iterations up front</span>
  <span class="k">var</span> n = <span class="n">0</span>
  <span class="k">while</span> (n &lt; <span class="n">100</span> &amp;&amp; !<span class="fn">done</span>()) &#123;
      n += <span class="fn">step</span>()
  &#125;

  <span class="c">// repeat — do this N times (cleaner than `for (i in 1..n)` when you don't need the index)</span>
  <span class="fn">repeat</span>(<span class="n">3</span>) &#123; <span class="fn">println</span>(<span class="s">"hi"</span>) &#125;   <span class="c">// hi, hi, hi</span></div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>No traditional C-style for loop.</strong> Kotlin doesn't have
        <code>for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)</code>. The reason: every use of that
        pattern is either "iterate a range" (use <code>for (i in 0 until n)</code>)
        or "iterate a collection" (use <code>for (x in xs)</code>). The middle
        form is gone.
      </div>

      <h3>break, continue, and labels</h3>

      <div class="code"><span class="code-tag">kotlin</span>
  <span class="c">// break exits the nearest loop. continue skips to the next iteration.</span>
  <span class="k">for</span> (u <span class="k">in</span> users) &#123;
      <span class="k">if</span> (u.banned) <span class="k">continue</span>
      <span class="k">if</span> (u.id == target) <span class="k">break</span>
  &#125;

  <span class="c">// Labels — break out of a NESTED loop. Rare, but useful.</span>
  <span class="k">outer@</span> <span class="k">for</span> (row <span class="k">in</span> grid) &#123;
      <span class="k">for</span> (cell <span class="k">in</span> row) &#123;
          <span class="k">if</span> (cell == target) <span class="k">break@</span><span class="v">outer</span>   <span class="c">// breaks the OUTER loop</span>
      &#125;
  &#125;</div>
    </div>

    <!-- 6. FUNCTIONS WITH DEFAULT + NAMED ARGS -->
    <h2><span class="num">6</span>functions — default args + named args</h2>

    <p>
      The last piece of ceremony: function calls. Kotlin lets you give parameters
      default values, and lets callers pass them by name. Together, this
      eliminates the need for overloads and removes "what was that second bool?"
      confusion at call sites.
    </p>

    <div class="concept" data-t="fns">
      <div class="concept-head">
        <div class="concept-icon">
          <svg width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M14 7h6v6"/><path d="M10 17 21 6"/><path d="M18 18 6 6l-3 3 12 12 3-3z"/></svg>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="concept-title">fun f(a: T, b: T = default, c: T = default): R</h3>
          <div class="concept-sub">declare defaults in the signature · call with names when it helps clarity</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="sig-viz">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">One function, many call shapes</h3>

        <div class="sig-def"><span class="k">fun</span> <span class="fn">connect</span>(
      host: <span class="t">String</span>,
      port: <span class="t">Int</span> = <span class="df">443</span>,
      timeoutMs: <span class="t">Long</span> = <span class="df">5_000</span>,
      retries: <span class="t">Int</span> = <span class="df">3</span>
  ): <span class="t">Connection</span></div>

        <div class="calls">
          <div class="call-row">
            <div class="call-code"><span class="fn">connect</span>(<span class="s">"api.x.com"</span>)</div>
            <div class="call-result">→ all defaults</div>
          </div>
          <div class="call-row">
            <div class="call-code"><span class="fn">connect</span>(<span class="s">"api.x.com"</span>, port = <span class="n">80</span>)</div>
            <div class="call-result">→ port overridden</div>
          </div>
          <div class="call-row">
            <div class="call-code"><span class="fn">connect</span>(<span class="s">"api.x.com"</span>, timeoutMs = <span class="n">10_000</span>, retries = <span class="n">5</span>)</div>
            <div class="call-result">→ skip port, override two</div>
          </div>
          <div class="call-row">
            <div class="call-code"><span class="fn">connect</span>(host = <span class="s">"api.x.com"</span>, port = <span class="n">80</span>)</div>
            <div class="call-result">→ all named</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="pair">
        <div>
          <div class="pair-label bad">Without defaults — overload explosion</div>
          <div class="code"><span class="c">// Four overloads to cover the combinations people actually call</span>
  <span class="k">fun</span> <span class="fn">connect</span>(host: <span class="t">String</span>): <span class="t">Connection</span>
  <span class="k">fun</span> <span class="fn">connect</span>(host: <span class="t">String</span>, port: <span class="t">Int</span>): <span class="t">Connection</span>
  <span class="k">fun</span> <span class="fn">connect</span>(host: <span class="t">String</span>, port: <span class="t">Int</span>, timeoutMs: <span class="t">Long</span>): <span class="t">Connection</span>
  <span class="k">fun</span> <span class="fn">connect</span>(host: <span class="t">String</span>, port: <span class="t">Int</span>, timeoutMs: <span class="t">Long</span>, retries: <span class="t">Int</span>): <span class="t">Connection</span></div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="pair-label good">With defaults — one signature</div>
          <div class="code"><span class="k">fun</span> <span class="fn">connect</span>(
      host: <span class="t">String</span>,
      port: <span class="t">Int</span> = <span class="n">443</span>,
      timeoutMs: <span class="t">Long</span> = <span class="n">5_000</span>,
      retries: <span class="t">Int</span> = <span class="n">3</span>
  ): <span class="t">Connection</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="concept-line">
        <strong>Named args are also self-documenting at the call site.</strong>
        Compare <code>connect("api.x.com", 443, 5000, 3)</code> with
        <code>connect("api.x.com", retries = 3)</code>. Same call, the second one
        tells you what the <code>3</code> means.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="warn">
      <strong>One rule with named args:</strong> once you start naming, you can't
      go back to positional for the same call. It's all-named or all-positional.
    </div>

    <!-- PICKING TOOLS -->
    <h2>Quick reference: which loop, which control flow?</h2>

    <div class="tree">
      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q1 · "I have a collection, I want to do something to each."</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">Use <code>for (x in xs)</code> or, more often, a collection op like <code>map</code> / <code>filter</code> / <code>forEach</code>.</div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ for (x in xs) &#123; ... &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q2 · "I need to repeat a fixed number of times."</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">Use <code>repeat(n) &#123; &#125;</code> if you don't need the index, or <code>for (i in 0 until n)</code> if you do.</div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ repeat(n) &#123; ... &#125; · for (i in 0 until n)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q3 · "I don't know iterations up front — depends on a condition."</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">Use <code>while</code>. The condition is the loop.</div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ while (cond) &#123; ... &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="tree-node">
        <div class="tree-q">Q4 · "I need to pick a value based on a condition."</div>
        <div class="tree-q-body">Use <code>if/else</code> or <code>when</code> as an expression. No temporary variable needed.</div>
        <div class="tree-ans">→ val x = if (cond) a else b</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- RECIPES -->
    <h2>Common patterns (copy these)</h2>

    <div class="recipes">
      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">val first, then var only if needed</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Default to val. Switch to var only when you actually reassign.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> name = <span class="fn">readName</span>()    <span class="c">// won't change</span>
  <span class="k">var</span> retries = <span class="n">0</span>           <span class="c">// will be reassigned in a loop</span>
  <span class="k">while</span> (retries &lt; <span class="n">3</span>) &#123;
      <span class="k">if</span> (<span class="fn">try</span>()) <span class="k">break</span>
      retries++
  &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">if/else as an expression</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">One line, no temporary variable, no ceremony.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> label = <span class="k">if</span> (score &gt;= <span class="n">60</span>) <span class="s">"pass"</span> <span class="k">else</span> <span class="s">"fail"</span>

  <span class="c">// Use as block form when each branch has multiple lines:</span>
  <span class="k">val</span> result = <span class="k">if</span> (x &gt; <span class="n">0</span>) &#123;
      <span class="fn">compute</span>()
      <span class="s">"positive"</span>
  &#125; <span class="k">else</span> &#123;
      <span class="fn">fallback</span>()
      <span class="s">"non-positive"</span>
  &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">when over an enum or sealed class</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Exhaustive matching — compiler ensures all cases are handled.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">val</span> text = <span class="k">when</span> (state) &#123;
      <span class="k">is</span> <span class="t">Loading</span> -&gt; <span class="s">"…"</span>
      <span class="k">is</span> <span class="t">Success</span> -&gt; <span class="s">"</span><span class="th">$&#123;</span>state.data.size<span class="th">&#125;</span><span class="s"> items"</span>
      <span class="k">is</span> <span class="t">Error</span>   -&gt; <span class="s">"oops: </span><span class="th">$&#123;</span>state.msg<span class="th">&#125;</span><span class="s">"</span>
  &#125;</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Loop with index (when you really need it)</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">`withIndex()` gives you `(index, element)` pairs.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="k">for</span> ((i, u) <span class="k">in</span> users.<span class="fn">withIndex</span>()) &#123;
      <span class="fn">println</span>(<span class="s">"</span><span class="th">$i</span><span class="s">: </span><span class="th">$u</span><span class="s">"</span>)
  &#125;
  <span class="c">// 0: User(...)</span>
  <span class="c">// 1: User(...)</span></div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Default + named at call site</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Override only what you need, name what isn't obvious.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="c">// fun connect(host, port=443, timeout=5000, retries=3)</span>
  <span class="fn">connect</span>(<span class="s">"api.x.com"</span>)                      <span class="c">// all defaults</span>
  <span class="fn">connect</span>(<span class="s">"api.x.com"</span>, retries = <span class="n">5</span>)         <span class="c">// skip ahead with names</span>
  <span class="fn">connect</span>(<span class="s">"api.x.com"</span>, port = <span class="n">80</span>, timeout = <span class="n">1000</span>)</div>
      </div>

      <div class="recipe">
        <div class="recipe-name">Repeat N times</div>
        <div class="recipe-desc">Cleaner than a for loop when you don't need the index.</div>
        <div class="code"><span class="fn">repeat</span>(<span class="n">3</span>) &#123;
      <span class="fn">println</span>(<span class="s">"loading…"</span>)
  &#125;

  <span class="c">// If you do need the index, use a range:</span>
  <span class="k">for</span> (i <span class="k">in</span> <span class="n">0</span> until <span class="n">3</span>) <span class="fn">println</span>(<span class="s">"step </span><span class="th">$i</span><span class="s">"</span>)</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- RECAP -->
    <div class="recap">
      <h2><span class="num">↺</span>Recap in one minute</h2>
      <p>
        Six features, one philosophy: <em>remove ceremony, leave intent.</em>
        Every feature on this page is a different place that philosophy shows up.
      </p>
      <div class="recap-grid">
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">val / var</div>
          <div class="desc"><code>val</code> by default — a read-only reference. <code>var</code> only when reality requires reassignment. Controls the binding, not necessarily the object.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">types</div>
          <div class="desc">Basic types: <code>Int</code>, <code>Long</code>, <code>Double</code>, <code>Boolean</code>, <code>String</code>, <code>Char</code>. Inference handles the rest. Annotate explicitly when it adds information.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">if / when / try as expressions</div>
          <div class="desc">Control flow <em>produces</em> a value. <code>val x = if (c) a else b</code>. No temporary variables, no separate return statements. <code>when</code> as expression is exhaustive.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">ranges</div>
          <div class="desc"><code>1..5</code> (inclusive), <code>1 until 5</code> (exclusive end), <code>1..10 step 2</code> (stride), <code>5 downTo 1</code> (reverse). <code>in</code>/<code>!in</code> are membership tests.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">loops</div>
          <div class="desc"><code>for (x in xs)</code> for any iterable. <code>while</code> for condition-driven loops. <code>repeat(n) &#123; &#125;</code> for N-times. No C-style for loop — the ceremony was always hiding a range or a collection.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="recap-item">
          <div class="lbl">functions: default + named args</div>
          <div class="desc">Defaults in the signature eliminate overloads. Named args at the call site self-document (<code>connect(retries = 3)</code>). One signature, many call shapes.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="end">
      One philosophy, six places it shows up: <em>if a piece of syntax only exists to
      satisfy the compiler, Kotlin removes it. What stays is intent.</em>
    </div>
</LessonLayout>

